+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<h2 id="mogu-crossref">Cross-Reference with Mogu Language</h2>
+
+<p>The Mogu enslaved the Pandaren for thousands of years, creating conditions for significant
+linguistic contact. The following table identifies potential shared elements between Pandaren
+and the Mogu language (see <a href="../mogu/vocabulary.md">Mogu vocabulary</a>).</p>
+
+<h3>Shared Morphemes</h3>
+
+<table>
+<tr><th>Element</th><th>Pandaren Attestation</th><th>Mogu Attestation</th><th>Proposed Origin</th><th>Confidence</th></tr>
+<tr>
+  <td>-shan</td>
+  <td>Mogu'shan (place name element)</td>
+  <td>Mogu'shan Palace, Mogu'shan Vaults</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">Likely Mogu origin; "shan" possibly = "palace" or "mountain"</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">Low</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td>Sha</td>
+  <td>The Sha (negative emotion entities)</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">(not clearly attested in Mogu)</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">Possibly from Shath'Yar or Old God vocabulary; neither Pandaren nor Mogu in origin</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">Low</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td>Lei</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">(not attested as Pandaren morpheme)</td>
+  <td>Lei Shen (Thunder King); possibly "thunder"</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">Likely Mogu</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">Low</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td>-jin</td>
+  <td>Huojin (philosophical faction)</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">(not clearly attested in Mogu)</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">Possibly Zandali borrowing into both languages</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">Low</td>
+</tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>Linguistic Contact Analysis</h3>
+
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Substrate influence:</strong> The Mogu dominated Pandaren culture for millennia.
+  Pandaren place names in the Vale of Eternal Blossoms and surrounding areas may preserve Mogu
+  toponyms. <em>Mogu'shan</em> is the clearest example: the element <em>-shan</em> likely
+  originates from Mogu, not Pandaren.</li>
+  <li><strong>Loan direction:</strong> Given the power dynamic, most loanwords likely flowed
+  from Mogu (dominant language) into Pandaren (substrate language). This is consistent with
+  real-world patterns of linguistic borrowing under domination.</li>
+  <li><strong>Sha vocabulary:</strong> The word <em>Sha</em> for the negative emotion entities
+  in Mists of Pandaria may be related to Shath'Yar (Old God language) rather than either
+  Pandaren or Mogu. The Sha are remnants of Y'Shaarj's power, which predates both races.</li>
+  <li><strong>Titan vocabulary:</strong> Both Pandaren and Mogu share exposure to Titan
+  facilities in Pandaria (Mogu'shan Vaults, Terrace of Endless Spring). Some place names
+  may have Titan-language origins that predate both peoples.</li>
+  <li><strong>Zandali influence:</strong> The Zandalari trolls invaded Pandaria and had
+  extensive contact with both Mogu and Pandaren. The element <em>-jin</em> may be a Zandali
+  borrowing present in both languages.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<div class="note">
+<strong>Methodological caveat:</strong> Both Pandaren and Mogu have extremely limited attestation.
+Cross-referencing two poorly attested languages multiplies uncertainty. The connections above
+are hypotheses, not confirmed relationships.
+</div>
+
 </body>
