-<h2 id="wordlist">Comparative Wordlist</h2>
+<h2 id="vrykul-drust">Vrykul &harr; Drust Cross-Reference</h2>
 
+<p>Drust is a daughter language of Vrykul, spoken by vrykul who settled Kul Tiras after the Sundering.
+Separated from mainland Vrykul for ~10,000 years, the Drust language developed unique innovations
+while retaining core Vrykul structural features. The Drust corpus is extremely small: no translated
+phrases exist, and analysis is based entirely on proper names.</p>
+
+<h3>Shared Elements (Inherited from Vrykul)</h3>
 <table>
+<tr><th>Element</th><th>Vrykul Form</th><th>Drust Form</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Right-headed compounding</td>
+  <td><em>Storm</em>+<em>heim</em> = Storm-home</td>
+  <td><em>Drust</em>+<em>var</em> = Drust-land (?)</td>
+  <td>modifier-head order</td>
+  <td>Inherited from Titan via Vrykul</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Regional suffix</td>
+  <td><em>-heim</em> (home/realm)</td>
+  <td><em>-var</em> (land/home?)</td>
+  <td>home / land / realm</td>
+  <td>May be cognate or independent innovation for same function</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Title + name pattern</td>
+  <td><em>Drottinn</em> Hrothgar</td>
+  <td><em>Gorak</em> Tul</td>
+  <td>title preceding personal name</td>
+  <td>"Gorak" may be the Drust cognate of "Drottinn" (lord)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Compound personal names</td>
+  <td>Bjarngrim, Mordvigbjorn</td>
+  <td>Lorkron, Gorak Tul</td>
+  <td>two-element names</td>
+  <td>Shared naming tradition</td>
+</tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>Drust Innovations (not from Vrykul)</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Element</th><th>Drust Form</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
+<tr><td><em class="inferred">Gol-</em></td><td>Gol Osigr, Gol Inath, Gol Koval, Gol Var</td><td>settlement / place / fortress (?)</td><td>Prefix; not found in any Vrykul dialect. Four consistent examples. Parallels Titan <em>Uld-</em> prefix.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>/ʒ/</em></td><td><em>Zhar</em> /ʒɑɾ/</td><td>(title element)</td><td>Voiced postalveolar fricative; not attested in Vrykul. May derive from Vrykul /ʃ/ voicing.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>/θ/</em> development</td><td><em>Thros</em> /θɾɔs/</td><td>(proper name; spirit realm)</td><td>/θ/ + /ɾ/ onset rare in Vrykul; Drust innovation.</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Second-syllable stress</td><td><em>Drustvar</em> /dɾʊstˈvɑɾ/</td><td>—</td><td>Diverges from Vrykul first-syllable stress; may be Common-language reanalysis.</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>Key Structural Divergence: Prefix vs. Suffix in Place Names</h3>
+
+<div class="note">
+The most significant typological difference between Drust and mainland Vrykul is the use of the
+<strong><em>Gol-</em> prefix</strong> for place names, whereas Vrykul uses <strong>suffixes</strong>
+(<em>-heim</em>, <em>-garde</em>, <em>-bron</em>). This is reminiscent of Titan facility naming
+(<em>Uld-</em> prefix: <em>Ulduar</em>, <em>Uldum</em>, <em>Uldaman</em>). The Drust may have
+preserved or reinvented a more Titan-like naming structure through their druidic connection to
+titan-forged origins. The Thornspeaker tradition (the uncorrupted druidic branch of Drust culture)
+may have maintained awareness of Titan naming patterns, while the corrupted Gorak Tul branch
+innovated new vocabulary for death magic.
+
+<table>
+<tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Language</th><th>Example</th><th>Structure</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Prefix</td><td>Titan</td><td><em>Ulduar</em></td><td><em>Uld-</em> + <em>-uar</em></td></tr>
+<tr><td>Prefix</td><td>Drust</td><td><em>Gol Osigr</em></td><td><em>Gol-</em> + root</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Suffix</td><td>Vrykul</td><td><em>Stormheim</em></td><td>Root + <em>-heim</em></td></tr>
+<tr><td>Suffix</td><td>Dwarven</td><td><em>Dun Morogh</em></td><td>Root + <em>-dun</em></td></tr>
+</table>
+</div>
+
+<h3>Potential Sound Correspondences: Vrykul &rarr; Drust</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Vrykul</th><th>Drust</th><th>Environment</th><th>Example</th><th>Confidence</th></tr>
+<tr><td>/ʃ/ (as in Gj- &rarr; /ʃ/)</td><td>/ʒ/</td><td>Postalveolar fricatives</td><td>Gjalerbron : Zhar</td><td class="uncertain">Low</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>-heim</em> /haɪm/</td><td><em>-var</em> /vɑɾ/</td><td>Place-name suffix</td><td>Stormheim : Drustvar</td><td class="uncertain">Very Low (functional parallel, not necessarily cognate)</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>-garde</em></td><td><em>Gol-</em></td><td>Place-naming strategy</td><td>Utgarde : Gol Osigr</td><td class="uncertain">Very Low (structural parallel, different morpheme)</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Initial consonant clusters</td><td>Reduced clusters</td><td>Word-initial position</td><td>Vr-, Sk- less common in Drust</td><td class="uncertain">Low</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<h2 id="titan-tolvir">Titan &harr; Tol'vir Cross-Reference</h2>
+
+<p>The Tol'vir were created by the titans to safeguard the facilities of Uldum. Their language is
+presumed to descend from Titan, though the attestation is extremely limited: three untranslated phrases,
+one confirmed noun (<em>khopesh</em> = "sickle sword"), three drill commands, and approximately 25 proper
+names. The Tol'vir use a hieroglyphic writing system and show heavy Egyptian-inspired naming conventions.</p>
+
+<h3>Shared Elements with Titan</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Element</th><th>Titan Form</th><th>Tol'vir Form</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td><em>-ir</em> / <em>-vir</em> suffix</td>
+  <td><em>Aesir</em>, <em>Vanir</em>, <em>Tol'vir</em></td>
+  <td><em>Tol'vir</em> (self-designation)</td>
+  <td>giant / being / animate</td>
+  <td>The Tol'vir name itself contains <em>-vir</em>, a phonologically conditioned variant of Titan <em>-ir</em></td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Apostrophe marking</td>
+  <td><em>Aman'Thul</em>, <em>Khaz'goroth</em>, <em>Val'anyr</em></td>
+  <td><em>Vir'naal</em>, <em>Vir'sar</em>, <em>Mar'at</em>, <em>Al'burq</em></td>
+  <td>morpheme boundary</td>
+  <td>Shared orthographic convention from Titan orthography</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Titan facility custodianship</td>
+  <td><em>Uldum</em> = Titan facility name</td>
+  <td>Tol'vir guard Uldum and the Halls of Origination</td>
+  <td>cultural connection</td>
+  <td>Tol'vir were created to maintain Titan facilities; they may retain Titan vocabulary for facility terms</td>
+</tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>Tol'vir Innovations (not from Titan)</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Element</th><th>Tol'vir Form</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Egyptian-inspired naming</td>
+<td><em>Neferset</em>, <em>Ankhaten</em>, <em>Tebhotep</em></td>
+<td>—</td>
+<td>Blizzard design choice; in-universe, an independent development</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Hieroglyphic script</td>
+<td>Confirmed by Tol'vir Hieroglyphic archaeology item</td>
+<td>writing system</td>
+<td>May derive from Titan iconography, but developed independently into hieroglyphs</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>Al'-</em> prefix</td>
+<td><em>Al'burq</em>, <em>Alra'ed</em></td>
+<td>definite article "the" (?)</td>
+<td>Resembles Arabic <em>al-</em>; may be a Tol'vir innovation or contact with elemental beings</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>Vir'-</em> prefix</td>
+<td><em>Vir'naal</em>, <em>Vir'sar</em></td>
+<td>water / river (?)</td>
+<td>Not attested in Titan; Tol'vir toponymic innovation</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>-et</em> / <em>-ep</em> suffix</td>
+<td><em>Amathet</em>, <em>Akhenet</em>, <em>Hamatep</em></td>
+<td>locative suffix (?)</td>
+<td>Not attested in Titan; possible Tol'vir grammatical innovation</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>khopesh</em></td>
+<td>/xoˈpɛʃ/</td>
+<td>sickle sword</td>
+<td>The only confirmed common noun; direct reference to Egyptian ḫpš</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>The <em>-vir</em> in Tol'vir: A Titan Inheritance</h3>
+
+<div class="note">
+The most secure linguistic connection between Tol'vir and Titan is the <em>-vir</em> element in
+"Tol'vir" itself. The Titan language uses <em>-ir</em> as an animate/giant suffix in <em>Aesir</em>
+(Storm Giants) and <em>Vanir</em> (Earth Giants). The Tol'vir self-designation <em>Tol'vir</em>
+contains <em>-vir</em>, which is best analyzed as a phonologically conditioned variant of the same
+Titan morpheme (the preceding liquid /l/ may have conditioned <em>-ir</em> &rarr; <em>-vir</em>,
+or <em>-vir</em> may preserve an older form). This makes Tol'vir the only titan-forged language
+other than Vrykul to retain the <em>-ir</em> animate suffix in its self-designation.
+
+<table>
+<tr><th>Race</th><th>Self-Designation</th><th>Morpheme</th><th>Gloss</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Storm Giants</td><td><em>Aesir</em></td><td><em>-ir</em></td><td>giant / being</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Earth Giants</td><td><em>Vanir</em></td><td><em>-ir</em></td><td>giant / being</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Tol'vir</td><td><em>Tol'vir</em></td><td><em>-vir</em></td><td>giant / being (?)</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Vrykul</td><td>(not retained in self-designation)</td><td><em>-kyr</em> (variant)</td><td>chooser / being (?)</td></tr>
+</table>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<h2 id="tolvir-mogu">Tol'vir &harr; Mogu Cross-Reference</h2>
+
+<p>Both Tol'vir and Mogu are titan-forged languages presumed to descend from Titan. Both races were
+created by titan keepers — the Mogu by Ra-den, the Tol'vir as custodians of Uldum under Ra's
+direction. They share historical contact: tol'vir ambassadors were sent to Lei Shen's court, and
+the Tol'vir activated the Forge of Origination against the Thunder King. Despite this contact, no
+lexical borrowings between Tol'vir and Mogu are attested in the limited corpus.</p>
+
+<h3>Shared Features (through common Titan descent)</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Tol'vir</th><th>Mogu</th><th>Titan Source</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td><em>-ir</em> animate suffix</td>
+  <td><em>Tol'vir</em></td>
+  <td>(not clearly retained)</td>
+  <td><em>Aesir</em>, <em>Vanir</em></td>
+  <td>Tol'vir retains <em>-vir</em>; Mogu has no clear reflex</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Apostrophe usage</td>
+  <td><em>Vir'naal</em>, <em>Al'burq</em></td>
+  <td><em>Lu'lin</em>, <em>Shan'ze</em></td>
+  <td><em>Aman'Thul</em>, <em>Khaz'goroth</em></td>
+  <td>Both mark morpheme boundaries, inherited from Titan orthography</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Ra-den connection</td>
+  <td>Created to guard Uldum under Ra's direction</td>
+  <td>Created by Ra-den</td>
+  <td>Ra-den = Highkeeper</td>
+  <td>Both races share Ra as creator; "Ra" may be a shared cultural loan from Titan</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Stone construct origins</td>
+  <td>Originally stone; some afflicted by Curse of Flesh</td>
+  <td>Originally stone; afflicted by Curse of Flesh</td>
+  <td>All titan-forged were stone/metal</td>
+  <td>Shared origin may explain shared deep vocabulary</td>
+</tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>Divergences</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Tol'vir</th><th>Mogu</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Naming aesthetic</td><td>Egyptian-inspired</td><td>Chinese-inspired</td><td>Completely different Blizzard design influences</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Writing system</td><td>Hieroglyphs</td><td>Could read/write Titan historically</td><td>Tol'vir developed own script; Mogu maintained Titan literacy</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Place-naming</td><td><em>-et</em>/<em>-ep</em> suffixes, <em>Vir'-</em> prefix</td><td><em>-shan</em> ("mountain"), <em>-dao</em> ("island")</td><td>Completely different toponymic systems</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Phonology</td><td>/ʃ/, /dʒ/, /h/ common; /a/-heavy</td><td>/ʃ/, /tʃ/, /ŋ/ common; tonal possible</td><td>Different sound inventories reflecting different design inspirations</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Confirmed vocabulary</td><td><em>khopesh</em>, <em>Kadesh</em>, <em>Osadi</em>, <em>Satekh</em></td><td><em>lei</em>, <em>shen</em>, <em>shan</em>, <em>dao</em></td><td>No shared confirmed words despite common Titan ancestry</td></tr>
+</table>
+
