+<h3>Toponymic Strategies Across the Family</h3>
+
+<div class="note">
+Each titan-forged language developed its own toponymic (place-naming) system, replacing or
+supplementing the Titan <em>Uld-</em> prefix pattern. The following table summarizes the strategies:
+
+<table>
+<tr><th>Language</th><th>Prefix Strategy</th><th>Suffix Strategy</th><th>Examples</th><th>Relationship to Titan</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Titan</td><td><em>Uld-</em> (facility)</td><td>Various (<em>-uar</em>, <em>-um</em>, <em>-aman</em>)</td><td><em>Ulduar</em>, <em>Uldum</em></td><td>Original system</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Vrykul</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><em>-heim</em>, <em>-garde</em>, <em>-bron</em>, <em>-korn</em></td><td><em>Stormheim</em>, <em>Utgarde</em></td><td>Replaced <em>Uld-</em> with Norse-style suffixes</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Drust</td><td><em>Gol-</em> (settlement)</td><td><em>-var</em> (land)</td><td><em>Gol Osigr</em>, <em>Drustvar</em></td><td>Preserved prefix pattern (like Titan); innovated <em>Gol-</em></td></tr>
+<tr><td>Dwarven</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><em>-dun</em>, <em>-modan</em>, <em>-batol</em></td><td><em>Dun Morogh</em>, <em>Grim Batol</em></td><td>Replaced <em>Uld-</em>; retained as loanword</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Mogu</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><em>-shan</em> (mountain)</td><td><em>Mogu'shan</em></td><td>Limited data; different naming tradition</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Tol'vir</td><td><em>Vir'-</em> (water?), <em>Al'-</em> (article?)</td><td><em>-et</em>/<em>-ep</em>, <em>-en</em></td><td><em>Vir'naal</em>, <em>Akhenet</em></td><td>Innovated own system; Egyptian-inspired</td></tr>
+</table>
+</div>
