+<h2 id="titan-gnomish">Titan &harr; Gnomish Cross-Reference</h2>
+
+<p>Gnomish descends from Titan via the Mechagnome lineage. Mechagnomes were clockwork
+titan-forged created by Keeper Mimiron, making Gnomish a sister language to Dwarven (via
+Earthen) and a cousin to Vrykul (via Iron Vrykul). The Gnomish corpus is extremely small:
+no Gnomish-language sentences are attested, and analysis is based entirely on proper names
+that may preserve Gnomish morphemes.</p>
+
+<h3>Shared Elements</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Element</th><th>Titan Form</th><th>Gnomish Form</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td><em>Mim-</em> / <em>Mek-</em></td>
+  <td><em>Mimiron</em> (Grand Architect Keeper)</td>
+  <td><em>Mekkatorque</em>, <em>Mekgineer</em>, <em>Mechagon</em>, <em>Mechagnome</em></td>
+  <td>creator / machine (?)</td>
+  <td>The phonological similarity between Titan <em>Mim-</em> and Gnomish <em>Mek-</em>/<em>Mech-</em> suggests a shared root. If <em>Mim-</em> = "architect/creator" in Titan, then <em>Mek-</em>/<em>Mech-</em> = "machine/craft" in Gnomish would represent a semantic split from a common ancestor: the creator and the creation share the same root. <strong>Confidence: Low.</strong></td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td><em>-ron</em> / <em>-iron</em></td>
+  <td><em>Mimiron</em>, <em>Khaz'goroth</em> (if *-roth*)</td>
+  <td><em>Mimiron</em> (borrowed), <em>Gorribal</em> (if *-ron* element)</td>
+  <td>honorific suffix (?)</td>
+  <td>The <em>-ron</em> element appears in Titan keeper names. Whether it was inherited in Gnomish as a productive suffix is unclear. <strong>Confidence: Very Low.</strong></td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Right-headed compounding</td>
+  <td><em>Gor</em>+<em>shalach</em> = dark-render</td>
+  <td><em>Mech</em>+<em>agnome</em> = machine-gnome</td>
+  <td>modifier-head order</td>
+  <td>Inherited compounding direction from Titan</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Place-name suffix pattern</td>
+  <td><em>Uld-</em> + suffix (<em>Ulduar</em>, <em>Uldum</em>)</td>
+  <td>Root + <em>-eregan</em>/<em>-agon</em> (<em>Gnomeregan</em>, <em>Mechagon</em>)</td>
+  <td>place/city designation</td>
+  <td>Gnomish uses suffix-based place naming (like Vrykul <em>-heim</em>, <em>-garde</em>), not prefix-based (like Titan <em>Uld-</em>). However, the suffixes <em>-eregan</em> and <em>-agon</em> may descend from Titan facility suffixes. <strong>Confidence: Very Low.</strong></td>
+</tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>Gnomish Innovations (not from Titan)</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Element</th><th>Gnomish Form</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
+<tr><td><em class="inferred">Gnom-</em></td><td><em>Gnomeregan</em>, <em>Gnome</em>, <em>Mechagnome</em></td><td>self-designation</td><td>Etymology uncertain; not attested in Titan or any other titan-forged language. May be a Gnomish innovation after the Curse of Flesh transformed mechagnomes into gnomes.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em class="inferred">Mek-</em> / <em>Mekk-</em> / <em>Mech-</em> / <em>Meka-</em></td><td>Multiple terms</td><td>"machine" / "mechanism" / "craft"</td><td>The phonological alternation *Mek-* ~ *Mekk-* ~ *Mech-* ~ *Meka-* is not clearly attested in Titan. If derived from Titan *Mim-*, it represents significant phonological innovation: *m* &rarr; *m* + velar, with gemination in the *Mekk-* variant.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em class="inferred">-torque</em></td><td><em>Mekkatorque</em></td><td>"title" / "rank" (?)</td><td>Not attested in any other titan-forged language; may be a Gnomish-specific title suffix.</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>Key Structural Comparison: Gnomish vs. Dwarven</h3>
+
+<div class="note">
+Gnomish and Dwarven are parallel Titan-forged descendants (via Mechagnomes and Earthen
+respectively). Both were affected by the Curse of Flesh and both coexisted in Ironforge
+for centuries. Key similarities and differences:
+
+<table>
+<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Gnomish</th><th>Dwarven</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Progenitor</td><td>Mechagnomes (Mimiron)</td><td>Earthen (Khaz'goroth?)</td><td>Different Keepers created different vocabulary domains</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Shared morpheme with Titan</td><td><em>Mek-</em>/<em>Mim-</em> (?)</td><td><em>Khaz-</em> (confirmed)</td><td>Each retained the name of their creator Keeper</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Place naming</td><td>Suffix: <em>-eregan</em>, <em>-agon</em></td><td>Suffix: <em>-dun</em>, <em>-modan</em>, <em>-batol</em></td><td>Both replaced Titan <em>Uld-</em> prefix with suffix-based naming</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Compounding</td><td>Right-headed (<em>Mech</em>+<em>agnome</em>)</td><td>Right-headed (<em>Grim</em>+<em>Batol</em>)</td><td>Both inherited from Titan</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Common bilingualism</td><td>Extensive</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Gnomish more heavily influenced by Common; most gnome names are Common compounds</td></tr>
+</table>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<h2 id="common-gnomish">Common &harr; Gnomish Cross-Reference</h2>
+
+<p>Common and Gnomish are parallel descendants of Titan through different lineages:
+Common via Vrykul &rarr; Humans, Gnomish via Mechagnomes &rarr; Gnomes. They are
+"distant cousins" in the titan-forged language family. Both have been heavily influenced
+by Common/English through bilingualism, making it difficult to identify native morphemes.</p>
+
+<h3>Shared Features (through common Titan ancestry)</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Common</th><th>Gnomish</th><th>Evidence</th></tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Right-headed compounding</td>
+  <td><em>Storm</em>+<em>wind</em>, <em>Strom</em>+<em>garde</em></td>
+  <td><em>Mech</em>+<em>agnome</em>, <em>Gnom</em>+<em>eregan</em></td>
+  <td>Inherited from Titan via different paths</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Fossilized Vrykul morphemes</td>
+  <td><em>-garde</em> (Stromgarde, Nethergarde)</td>
+  <td>None clearly attested</td>
+  <td>Common retained Vrykul morphemes; Gnomish did not (different lineage)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td>Heavy Common/English influence</td>
+  <td>Rendered as English in-game</td>
+  <td>Most gnome names are Common compounds</td>
+  <td>Both languages have been overwhelmed by Common/English vocabulary</td>
+</tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>Divergence Factors</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Common</th><th>Gnomish</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Lineage</td><td>Titan &rarr; Vrykul &rarr; Common</td><td>Titan &rarr; Mechagnome &rarr; Gnomish</td>
+    <td>Different intermediary languages; Common retains Vrykul elements, Gnomish retains Mechagnome/Titan elements</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Creator Keeper</td><td>No single Keeper (humans descended from vrykul)</td><td>Mimiron (Grand Architect)</td>
+    <td>Gnomish may preserve Mimiron-related vocabulary; Common does not</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Fossilized morphemes</td><td><em>-garde</em>, <em>Tiris-</em>, <em>-fal</em></td><td><em>Mek-</em>/<em>Mech-</em>, <em>Gnom-</em></td>
+    <td>Different fossilized elements reflecting different ancestral paths</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Phonological simplification</td><td>Significant (from Vrykul)</td><td>Significant (from Mechagnome/Titan)</td>
+    <td>Both simplified from their ancestors, but in different ways</td></tr>
+</table>
+
