+<h2 id="nefer-connection">The Nefer- Element: Nerubian &harr; Tol'vir</h2>
+
+<div class="note">
+<strong>Assessment:</strong> The <em>Nefer-</em> element in nerubian <em>Neferess</em> and Tol'vir <em>Neferset</em>
+is almost certainly a shared Blizzard design inspiration from Egyptian <em>nfr</em> ("beautiful/good"),
+not a genuine in-universe linguistic connection. No other morphemes are shared between the two languages.
+<strong>Confidence: Very Low</strong> for genuine connection; <strong>High</strong> for shared design inspiration.
+</div>
+
+<table>
+<tr><th>Name</th><th>Language</th><th>Context</th><th>Decomposition</th></tr>
+<tr><td><em>Neferess</em></td><td>Nerubian</td><td>Former queen of Azj-Kahet</td><td class="inferred">*Nefer-* + *-ess*</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>Neferset</em></td><td>Tol'vir</td><td>Tol'vir tribe in Uldum</td><td class="inferred">*Nefer-* + *-set*</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>Both forms share the exact sequence /nɛfɛr/. If drawn from Egyptian <em>nfr</em> ("beautiful"):
+<em>Neferess</em> &asymp; "the beautiful one"; <em>Neferset</em> &asymp; "beautiful place."
+However, the completely different suffix systems (*-ess* vs. *-set*) and lack of any other shared
+morphemes between nerubian and Tol'vir strongly suggest independent Blizzard design choices.</p>
+
+<h2 id="ahn-azj">The Ahn- vs. Azj- Alternation</h2>
+
+<p>The place names <em>Ahn'kahet</em> (WotLK) and <em>Azj-Kahet</em> (TWW) share *-kahet*
+but differ in the initial element. Possible explanations:</p>
+
+<table>
+<tr><th>Hypothesis</th><th>Analysis</th><th>Plausibility</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Different morphemes</td><td>*Ahn-* = "old" (if Ahn'kahet = Old Kingdom); *Azj-* = "deep" (if Azj-Kahet = Deep Kingdom)</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Dialectal variation</td><td>Proto-nerubian *\*Anzj-* &rarr; Northrend *Ahn-* / Khaz Algar *Azj-*</td><td>Low&ndash;Medium</td></tr>
+<tr><td>*Ahn-* is not nerubian</td><td>*Ahn'kahet* may be an Aqir/qiraji name (cf. *Ahn'qiraj*); *Azj-Kahet* is nerubian</td><td>Low&ndash;Medium</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Confidence: Low.</strong> Data insufficient to distinguish hypotheses.</p>
+
 </body>
 </html>

