-<p>The surface variation may reflect a single underlying suffix *-ia* /jə/ with phonological conditioning, or the common element may be final *-a* (a feminine suffix) with the preceding vowel belonging to the stem. <strong>Note on gender discrepancy:</strong> warcraft.wiki.gg uses male icons for both *Ruia* and *On'hiea* — if these characters are male, the *-ia* feminine suffix analysis is weakened. No male Haranir names end in *-a*. The *-a* feminine ending is a widespread Blizzard design pattern across WoW languages (Draenei, Nazja), making it difficult to distinguish genuine morphology from naming aesthetics. The *-neia* ending in *Shay'neia* parallels *Ney'leia* — whether *-neia* / *-leia* is a variant of *-ia* or a different morpheme remains unresolved.</p>
+<p>The surface variation may reflect a single underlying suffix *-ia* /jə/ with phonological conditioning, or the common element may be final *-a* (a feminine suffix) with the preceding vowel belonging to the stem. <strong>Note on gender discrepancy:</strong> warcraft.wiki.gg uses male icons for both *Ruia* and *On'hiea* — if these characters are male, the *-ia* feminine suffix analysis is weakened. No male Haranir names end in *-a*. The *-a* feminine ending is a widespread Blizzard design pattern across WoW languages (Draenei, Nazja), making it difficult to distinguish genuine morphology from naming aesthetics.</p>
 
-<p><strong>Confidence: Very Low–Low.</strong></p>
+<p><strong>Decomposition analysis:</strong> The four endings are most parsimoniously analyzed as <strong>name stems + final *-a* (feminine suffix)</strong>, rather than as four distinct suffixes:</p>
 
+<table>
+<tr><th>Name</th><th>Ending</th><th>Stem + *-a*</th><th>Stem</th></tr>
+<tr><td>*Shay'neia*</td><td>*-neia*</td><td>*Shay'nei* + *-a*</td><td>*-nei*</td></tr>
+<tr><td>*Ney'leia*</td><td>*-leia*</td><td>*Ney'lei* + *-a*</td><td>*-lei*</td></tr>
+<tr><td>*On'hiea*</td><td>*-iea*</td><td>*On'hi* + *-a*</td><td>*-i*</td></tr>
+<tr><td>*Ruia*</td><td>*-ia*</td><td>*Rui* + *-a*</td><td>*-i*</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>The variation is in the stem (*-nei*, *-lei*, *-i*), not in the suffix. The single suffix *-a* is more parsimonious than three or four phonologically conditioned variants of *-ia*.</p>
+
+<p><strong>Confidence: Very Low–Low.</strong> The *-neia* data point from *Shay'neia* is consistent with but does not strengthen the hypothesis.</p>
+
 <h3 id="nerubian-proximity">Haranir–Nerubian Geographic Proximity</h3>
 
 <p>Wildcamp Ul'ar is located near the City of Threads in Azj-Kahet, placing Haranir and nerubians in direct geographic proximity. The *-ar* element in <span class="inferred">*Ul'ar*</span> parallels the nerubian <span class="inferred">*-ar*</span> faction suffix (Anub'ar, Nerub'ar, Hath'ar). If *-ar* were a nerubian loanword, the semantic shift from "brood/collective" to "camp/settlement" would be natural. However, *-ar* is a common syllable, and no other Haranir–nerubian shared morphemes exist.</p>
 

